ef 1071 "Let $T: R^{3} → R^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $Im(T) = {[x, y, z]^{T}: 3x + 2y - z = 0}$ then $nullity(T) = 1$\n2. If $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx = [1,-2, 1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible\n3. If $v_{1}$, $v_{2}$, and $v_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and $v_{3} = v_{1} + 2v_{2}$, then $Ker(T) = {0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186188931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b3a8f74-a4d2-5020-9ea6-4077acac1b2e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189066 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189094 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189066 #edb/ref 1061 20021 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189066 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b289521-18f1-582d-b604-520f11ba29d7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189066 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188267 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189083 #edb/ref 1061 26827 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189083 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "626e026a-01da-503f-a127-2b353dbc209b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189083 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188279 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189084 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189066 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189084 #edb/ref 1061 26840 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189084 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e0c933b-d880-5bfa-804a-944d21dae8a3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189084 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189371 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189094 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189083 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189094 #edb/ref 1061 37613 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189094 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6735a644-2990-5e5c-b639-afb52f0b4d15" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189094 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188287 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189371 #edb/ref 1064 "The **rank-nullity theorem in terms of linear transformations** states the following:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation, then* $$\\textrm{dim(Im}(\\mathbf{T})) + \\textrm{dim(Ker}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),$$ *or equivalently,* $$\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = n.$$\n\nFor example, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$ is a linear transformation such that $$\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{ [ x, y, z ]^T \\, : \\, y+2z=0\\}.$$\n\nLet's use the rank-nullity theorem to compute $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})$ and $\\text{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$\n\nFirst, solving $y+2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y = -2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ can be written as $$\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis means that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}.$ And since $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are linearly independent, we have $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=2.$\n\nFinally, using the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain $$\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\ & =3-2 \\\\ & =1.\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189371 #edb/ref 1065 4379 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189371 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9c4215af-a9cf-5a1a-aabc-40c5395f2df4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186189371 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585]}
